add('''<p>The first two studies can differ without contradiction: company populations, periods, thresholds and the distinction between “meet/exceed” and “exceed” differ. They motivate competing operational hypotheses, not a meta-analytic rare-disease bias estimate. The earlier 2026 rare-launch article lead remains access-limited and is not upgraded to verified full-text evidence.</p>
<h2 id="analogues">Choose an analogue for an assumption</h2>
<p>A single TAM number is an inadequate similarity measure. Compare <strong>eligible diagnosed patients, incident versus prevalent pools, age/function window, treatment frequency, clinical benefit, center capacity, payer mix, net price and competitive durability</strong>. A smaller IV enzyme market can be useful for patient finding while a larger intrathecal ASO market can be useful for procedure economics. Neither supplies an all-purpose forecast curve.</p>
<p>Efficacy should be described in patient-relevant dimensions: survival, seizure burden, functional gain or preservation, magnitude, onset, durability, safety/tolerability, response heterogeneity and evidence quality. Percent changes on unrelated scales cannot be normalized into a credible common efficacy score. Preclinical rescue supports biological plausibility and scenario design; it does not estimate human willingness to start, persistence or developmental reversibility.</p>
<p><strong>Population-size matching remains incomplete.</strong> The 2004 analyst estimated worldwide affected populations of about 1,100 for MPS VI, 2,000 for MPS II and 5,000–10,000 for Pompe. Those are historical beliefs, not current eligible US markets. Ztalmy's company-reported >165 active patients at year-end2023 is a treatment count, not disease prevalence. These denominators cannot be ranked against a SYNGAP1 advocacy census as if they were equivalent TAMs. The data retain accessible US population as unknown until date, genotype, age, geography and eligibility are matched; no falsely precise TAM-distance score is assigned.</p>''')
